	affiliations = {Institute of International Relations and Political Science, Vilnius University, Vilnius, Lithuania},
	abstract = {The Baltic States shape their approach to drone technologies, driven by Ukraine’s experience in the battlefield, amid the EU’s discussions on digital sovereignty. Although small states are seen as having limited capacity, they view drones, less constrained by structural asymmetries, as a transformative power element to counter a hostile neighbour. This article examines how the Baltic States construct their approach to drones as a response to immediate security needs. Through the discourse analysis of political elites’ articulations, the article shows that drones are framed not only as military assets but as instruments of national subjectivity and self-reliance. The article reveals that drones are widely embraced and hyped, where societal involvement further blurs the lines between the civilian and military spheres. The article contributes to debates on digital sovereignty and the security of small states by highlighting the contrast in how security and sovereignty are understood. The EU focuses on industry, regulation and global competition, projecting its power and governance both internally and externally. Meanwhile, the Baltic States prioritise drones as building military capacity, emphasising survival, territorial integrity and political independence. These positions challenge the centre-periphery dynamics between Brussels and smaller member states, questioning the link between power and subordination. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
	author_keywords = {Baltic States; Digital sovereignty; drone technologies; security; small states},
