	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI), with its growing impact on governance and societal issues, has become a central focus in global public policy research, particularly in regulation, data governance, and ethics. This study analyzes trends and emerging hotspots from 2014 to 2024 using bibliometric methods on 616 journal articles and 45 review papers indexed in Web of Science, utilizing CiteSpace and VOSviewer. Findings reveal that: (1) AI public policy research has steadily expanded, with core hotspots in algorithmic governance, data transparency, and AI ethics; (2) the United States and United Kingdom lead AI policy research, with institutions like Oxford University significantly contributing; (3) a clear thematic shift has occurred, moving from privacy protection and regulation toward ethical considerations, transparency, and public engagement; and (4) future AI governance frameworks are anticipated to emphasize data accountability and international collaboration to address evolving policy challenges. © 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
