	affiliations = {Open Government Partnership (OGP), United Kingdom; South Africa; Faculty of Laws, University College London, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {When governments regulate and use data and algorithms for decision-making, they may increase or decrease risks to individuals and communities. In order to maximize the benefits and minimize risks, policymakers around the world are introducing a variety of tools to provide safeguards for innovation. These tools aim to limit individual harms such as privacy violations, as well as collective harms such as misinformation or environmental damage. A number of these safeguards involve public oversight and accountability as an additional layer of oversight beyond government-only regulation. Approaches include establishing basic mandates for public institutions to specific regimes for public disclosure, audit, and inspection of data use. At the time of writing, consensus has not emerged about what the best practice models are for disclosure - whether algorithmic transparency registers, human rights impact assessments, consent-based transparency, or some combination thereof. Nonetheless, such models will need to be a part of the regulatory mixture by helping conserve regulatory resources and allow for continued innovation. © The Editor and Contributing Authors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
