	abstract = {It might seem that the global threat posed by the hybrid non-agentic/ agentic nature of artificial intelligence (AI) would encourage governments to jointly regulate it. Although there have been successful instances of international cooperation aimed at averting a catastrophe, leading nations are in no hurry to assume commitments that would limit their growth, which is now thought to be increasingly dependent on AI. Most states’ acts related to AI development include sections on ethical regulations, which reveal differing normative approaches that make discussions about AI ethics an important element of interstate discursive competition. Russia’s significant lag behind the U.S., China, and the EU does not preclude its possible emergence as a discursive competitor. © 2025, Foreign Policy Research Foundation. All rights reserved.},
