	affiliations = {Inti International University, Nilai, 71800, Malaysia},
	abstract = {Malaysia's transition to a digitally driven, high-income economy hinges on safeguarding sovereignty over data, infrastructure, and algorithms while enabling innovation and cross-border trade. This paper proposes an integrated governance architecture aligning Malaysia's Personal Data Protection Act 2010 (PDPA), the Malaysia Digital Economy Blueprint (MyDIGITAL), the Malaysia Cyber Security Strategy (MCSS) 2020-2024 and the new Cyber Security Act 2024 with ASEAN frameworks (ASEAN Data Management Framework, ADMF) and international standards (ISO/IEC 27001:2022, ISO/IEC 27701, NIST Privacy Framework) to operationalize "digital sovereignty by design"across public services, critical infrastructure, and emerging immersive ecosystems. We introduce a policy-tech stack that couples privacy-preserving data sharing, sovereign cloud controls, verifiable cross-border transfers, and measured outcomes using a sovereignty scorecard. A multi-sector methodology (energy, health, public sector) demonstrates how to implement controls, validate compliance, and quantify benefits (reduced breach risk, faster data-sharing SLAs, improved trust indices). Results include a maturity baseline for Malaysian ministries and GLCs, gap analyses versus ASEAN DMF, and a practical roadmap for 24-month adoption. The incident rates show medium-severity incidents per 1,000 users dropped by 28-41% across pilots. DPIA coverage increased from 38% to >85%, supported by standardized DPIA templates. © 2025 IEEE.},
