	abstract = {Although education has rapidly adopted the use of artificial intelligence (AI), questions of fairness, responsibility, and how to reach ethical decisions are difficult. A systematic review was conducted of 44 peer-reviewed papers (2013-2025) that were identified in the Web of Science using the PRISMA guidelines. In addition, bibliometric mapping and thematic synthesis were conducted. The descriptive registrations show an exponential increase in the number of publications (29.4% yearly), with a sharp increase seen after 2022. Most publications took place within the context of the institutions of the Anglophone and European countries. The articles represented work in the areas of computer science, applied ethics, medicine, and education and focused on four key themes: algorithmic fairness, transparency and accountability, and bioethical responsibility within the context of generative AI in teaching. Despite some progress, the field remains immature, empirically weak, geographically narrow, and lacking in interdisciplinary frameworks. This review integrates the current state of knowledge and looks forward by providing further directions for future research. It provides a firm foundation for scholars, policymakers, and academic leaders to advance the ethical use of AI in higher education. © 2025 IEEE},
