	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037972514?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Zarqa University, Department of Computer Science, Zarqa, Jordan},
	abstract = {The adoption of large-scale artificial intelligence (AI) models has tightened the link between digital sovereignty and access to high-performance computing infrastructure. For emerging economies like the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan this poses both opportunity and risk. On the one hand, AI promises to transform sectors ranging from fintech and e-commerce to health and logistics, and Jordanian startups are already deploying chatbots, fraud detection and personalised recommendation systems to improve efficiency and customer experience. On the other hand, reliance on foreign hyperscale clouds can undermine data sovereignty, export capital and constrain national innovation. This paper delivers a data-grounded blueprint for a national data centre (NDC) and sovereign AI platform tailored to Jordan's economic and geopolitical context. Our design integrates detailed local metrics-such as the country's eight existing data centres (seven in Amman and one in Aqaba), electricity tariffs for large industry (0.17 USD · kWh1), renewable energy contributing roughly 27-30% of generation, the presence of 20 universities offering AI programmes to about 3000 students, and Jordan's commitment to train 15000 people and launch 25 government AI projects by 2027. We present a modular, pod-based architecture with secure sovereign enclaves, high-density AI pods and support for regional service delivery. A total cost of ownership (TCO) model grounded in Jordan-specific data shows that a 50 -rack phase-one facility would require roughly 25.7 M in capital expenditure and 3.5 M in annual operating expenditure, yielding a five-year TCO around 43.4 M; energy costs dominate long-term OPEX. We examine sensitivity to power-usage-effectiveness (PUE), electricity tariffs and GPU pricing. Finally, we map governance recommendations to existing national bodies defined in the Jordanian AI Strategy and illustrate the platform's impact through local startup case studies, including Repzo's AI-enabled CRM with more than 2,500 representatives and Abjjad's e-reading service with 1.5 million registered users. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AI infrastructure; Cloud computing; Cost modelling; Data centres; Data governance; Energy efficiency; Jordan; Sovereign AI},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Costs; Data centers; Electronic commerce; Government data processing; Green computing; Information systems; International trade; Network security; Artificial intelligence infrastructure; Cloud-computing; Cost models; Data governances; Datacenter; Electricity tariff; Energy; Jordan; Sovereign artificial intelligence; Total cost of ownership; Cloud computing},
