	abstract = {The proliferation of autonomous decision systems in areas of healthcare, finance, defence, and transportation is deeply ethically problematic in the areas of transparency, accountability, and fairness. Classic AI models will put a strong emphasis on the performance and scalability, but fail to consider the ethical and social consequences of autonomous decision-making. The present paper suggests an Ethical AI Framework of Autonomous Decision Systems, which combines fairness, explainability and human control concepts with adherence to changing legal standards. The framework consists of a multi-level design that integrates the modules of ethical reasoning, bias detection pipelines, and accountability systems to provide responsible autonomy. The proposed framework reduces the risk of unintended harm even though it maintains efficiency in the system, as normative constraints are introduced into the decision-making loop. Based on case studies and experimental validations, we have shown that the protection of ethics increases trust, decreases bias, and permits compliance with human values under complicated settings. The article helps fill the gap between technical efficiency and ethical responsibility, offering a starting point towards creating next-generation autonomous systems which are both intelligent and morally responsible. © 2025 IEEE.},
