	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021600406?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This chapter explores Responsible artificial intelligence (AI) implementation in major online marketplaces, focusing on transparency, accountability, inclusivity, and sustainability. Companies use Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) reports without universal AI standards to showcase ethical practices. By analyzing reports from Amazon, eBay, Allegro, Alibaba, and Uber, the study examines how these platforms incorporate Trustworthy AI principles, as outlined by the EU’s “AI Act,” into their systems. ESG reports are foundational for sustainability reporting, yet companies differ in their approaches to explaining AI-related topics, highlighting the need for standardized practices. The findings reveal that while platforms use AI technologies like recommendation engines, chatbots, and fraud detection to enhance user experience, the application of ethical principles varies widely. Implementing Responsible AI in online marketplaces requires collaboration among diverse stakeholders, combining technical expertise with business ethics. This chapter aims to achieve greater clarity and consistency in Responsible AI adoption, contributing to AI’s transparency, trust, and ethical standards. The research supports sustainable development in the e-commerce sector by fostering responsible practices. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Ewa Wanda Ziemba, Wioletta Grzenda and Michał Ramsza; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.},
