	abstract = {As some of the biggest producers and users of AI, corporations must remain front of mind for discussions about AI policy. The key legal challenge concerning corporate AI development is how to structure governance mechanisms that incentivize socially beneficial AI while minimizing risks. Two alternative regimes have emerged: a regulatory approach that requires corporations to develop AI according to pre-set safety standards and a liability approach that simply forces corporations to pay for harms their AI (however developed) causes. The central legal question concerning corporate use of AI is how to adapt twentieth-century laws that assume corporations only act through people to a new world in which corporations increasingly act through algorithms. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
