	journal = {2025 IEEE 6th KhPI Week on Advanced Technology, KhPIWeek 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/KhPIWeek61436.2025.11288644},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031782274?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Lviv Polytechnic National University, Department of Information Protection, Lviv, Ukraine},
	abstract = {The development of effective mechanisms for controlling ethical behavior in AI-powered machines remains a critical challenge in artificial intelligence research. This paper presents a novel approach to preventing AI systems from exceeding the boundaries of permissible ethical actions through a dual-algorithm mechanism that mirrors human behavioral patterns. The proposed method introduces an innovative concept based on the symbiosis of two fundamental algorithms: one that induces system disruption in response to unethical actions (analogous to human suffering), and another that maintains operational continuity (similar to human avoidance of suffering). This approach represents a significant departure from traditional ethics enforcement methods in AI systems, which typically rely solely on predefined rules or utility functions. The core contribution of this research lies in its biomimetic approach to AI ethics control, drawing parallels between human behavioral mechanisms and machine control systems. By implementing a system that combines disruption and continuity algorithms, we create a self-regulating mechanism that not only prevents unethical actions but also ensures system stability and operational reliability. This paper describes the theoretical framework underlying the proposed method, provides models for both algorithms, and demonstrates their practical implementation through detailed examples. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AI safety; algorithmic symbiosis; artificial intelligence; autonomous systems; ethical boundaries; machine ethics},
	keywords = {Behavioral research; Biomimetics; Ethical technology; Man machine systems; System stability; AI safety; AI systems; Algorithmic symbiosis; Algorithmics; Autonomous system; Effective mechanisms; Ethical behavior; Ethical boundary; Machine ethic; Unethical actions; Artificial intelligence},
