	affiliations = {SUNY, University at Albany, College of Emergency Preparedness and Homeland Security (CEHC), Albany, United States},
	abstract = {Organizations deploying artificial intelligence systems globally face the complexity of privacy regulations that vary significantly across multiple jurisdictions. This research investigates the practical challenges these organizations encounter when attempting to comply with multiple regulatory frameworks simultaneously. Through an empirical assessment methodology, we evaluate a representative AI customer analytics platform against five major privacy regimes: the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California's Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) and Consumer Privacy Rights Act (CPRA), Brazil's General Data Protection Law (LGPD), China's Personal Information Protection Law (PIPL), and the European Union's AI Act. The analysis reveals substantial compliance challenges in several critical domains, including contradictory transparency expectations, varying human oversight requirements, inconsistent data minimization principles, complex cross-border transfer restrictions, and divergent risk assessment methodologies. The quantitative examination shows meaningful overlap between regulatory frameworks (ranging from 43 % to 60 % between GDPR and other regulations); as such, we advocate for streamlined approaches that also highlight the jurisdiction-specific requirements (5-33%), necessitating tailored controls. Between 5% and 33% of jurisdictionspecific criteria require customized controls tailored to local requirements. Based on these findings, we propose strategies for global AI privacy compliance and policy recommendations to address regulatory fragmentation. © 2025 IEEE.},
