	affiliations = {University of Marburg, Germany; IU Internationale Hochschule, Aachen, Germany},
	abstract = {This chapter explores how global artificial intelligence (AI) governance strategies differ across major world regions and their implications for higher education. It highlights the rapid development of AI technologies and the varying governance approaches in the USA, the EU, and China. Using a Foucauldian power/knowledge perspective, it identifies three main AI governance strategies: the free play of forces (USA), regulation (EU), and control (China). These strategies reflect broader societal, political, and economic conditions that influence policy and organizational practices in higher education. The USA's market-driven approach encourages innovation, while the EU emphasizes ethical AI, trustworthiness, and data protection. China's AI strategy aligns closely with state control and national interests. The chapter discusses how AI disrupts knowledge production and educational practices, calling for a balanced approach that fosters innovation while addressing ethical concerns. It concludes with reflections on the future role of AI in higher education governance. © The Editors and Contributing Authors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Artificial Intelligence Governance; Discursive Organizing; Ethical AI; Higher Education; Power/Knowledge Dynamics; Regulatory Strategies},
