	pages = {155 - 164},
	doi = {10.52202/083105-0015},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032959376?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {University of Moratuwa, Sri Lanka; Ispace, inc., South Korea},
	abstract = {As artificial intelligence (AI) becomes increasingly integrated into mission control, autonomous spacecraft navigation, and robotic operations, the ethical implications of human-AI collaboration in space decision-making have become a critical concern. AI systems now play a vital role in data processing, real-time anomaly detection, autonomous repairs, and deep-space mission planning. However, accountability, transparency, and ethical oversight remain underexplored. The risk of biased decision-making, unforeseen system failures, and the potential sidelining of human judgment raises questions about how AI should be governed in critical space operations. This study explores the ethical dimensions of AI-driven decision-making in space exploration by examining case studies from Mars rovers, autonomous satellite operations, and mission-critical AI applications. We investigate key issues such as decision accountability in autonomous AI actions, risk mitigation strategies for mission-critical AI failures, and ensuring fairness and bias reduction in AI-generated mission directives. Additionally, we assess the role of explainable AI (XAI) in making AI-driven decisions more interpretable and trustworthy for mission controllers. Furthermore, this research proposes a framework for AI governance in space, emphasizing human-in-the-loop oversight models, AI ethics protocols, and international policy recommendations for AI reliability in deep-space missions. By addressing these ethical challenges, this study aims to contribute to the development of trustworthy AI systems that enhance, rather than replace, human decision-making in space while ensuring safety, accountability, and ethical integrity in future space missions. Copyright ©2025 by the International Astronautical Federation (IAF). All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {AI Accountability; AI Ethics; AI Governance; Autonomous Systems; Deep-Space Missions; Explainable AI; Human-in-the-Loop Oversight; Space Decision-Making},
