	affiliations = {UCL, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Concerning the prevailing hegemonic global governance discourse on global digital transformation policy (GDTP) and public-sector digital transformation (PSDT) initiatives, a decolonial turn is needed. This chapter proposes a decolonial global political economy of digital transformation (GPEDT) framework interrogating hegemonic global governance narratives on GDTP's contradictions, tensions, and omissions across three overarching policy pillars: digital public infrastructures, digital markets, and data governance. Also, the GPEDT framework presents the coloniality of global digital transformation (CGDT) concept to illuminate GDTP's entanglements with broader drivers of structural inequality—increasing data value extraction, diminishing data value creation capabilities, and eroding digital sovereignty. GDTP's intersections with structural imperialism and coloniality and its materially (and disproportionately) harmful implications for the Global South are examined through two examples. The GPEDT unmasks the CGDT and its adverse implications through its more expansive and intersectional framework and thus can do more to actively diminish structural imperialism and violence by cultivating a more just approach to GDTP and PSDT. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
