	abstract = {On the grounds of a literature review in media and journalism studies, this chapter aims to provide an overview of the ethical implications regarding the use of algorithmic systems in news media and journalism, as well as how and to what extent these issues are being addressed. First, it presents an overview of the literature that has addressed artificial intelligence (AI) ethics in general and how ethical concerns are dealt with in journalism (studies). The chapter then examines how the emergence of generative AI has changed the discussions revolving around algorithmic accountability and has thus made ethical considerations related to AI systems more pressing both in national and international policy debates (e.g., in the European Union) and in self-regulatory approaches of the news industry. It then moves on to an analysis of central ethical principles such as transparency, accountability, and diversity related to algorithmic systems. The chapter concludes with a discussion of AI governance in journalism and news media. © 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc.},
