	affiliations = {USC-SJTU Institute of Cultural and Creative Industry, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China; College of Journalism and Communications, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL, United States},
	abstract = {This longitudinal study advances the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) by integrating AI ethics as a dynamic mediator linking social influence to technology acceptance. Drawing on a two-wave panel survey of U.S. adults (W1 = 2010; W2 = 942), the research demonstrates that social persuasion, defined as the extent to which an individual’s social network influences their use of AI, significantly shapes AI ethics. These perceptions, in turn, enhance perceived ease of use and usefulness, which ultimately foster more favorable attitudes toward AI. The study makes notable contributions to TAM literature. First, it incorporates AI ethics as a critical mechanism through which social influence affects the acceptance of AI. Second, employing a longitudinal design, it demonstrates that AI ethics is not static but evolves continuously under social influence, thus providing deeper insights beyond the limitations of traditional cross-sectional studies. Lastly, by redefining AI acceptance as a socially constructed ethical process rather than a mere cognitive evaluation of technology utility, the research highlights actionable policy and practical strategies for leveraging social networks to cultivate ethical consensus and promote responsible AI adoption. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
