	abstract = {This article examines the efforts of Arab nations to achieve digital sovereignty amidst the global dominance of the United States in the digital economy. It argues that while Arab countries have prioritized digital technology independence and data governance as key components of their development strategies, they encounter significant challenges due to the pervasive cultural, economic, political, and military influence of the United States. These obstacles manifest in limited access to digital resources, restricted participation in the global digital market, and insufficient representation in digital governance structures. In response, most Arab nations are increasingly adopting a strategy of ‘independent innovation’ combined with collaboration, particularly through partnerships with China. The article draws upon the conceptual frameworks of digital sovereignty and digital imperialism and employs a process-tracing methodology—that integrates with Dependency Theory—to examine Arab states’ efforts to secure digital sovereignty in an interconnected global economic system. The main contribution, however, remains in analyzing the Arab region’s effort to pursue digital sovereignty by specifically examining China’s role in fostering Arab digital independence as part and parcel of Arab national security. © 2025 Editors of Middle East Critique.},
