	abstract = {The rapid evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure is reshaping global economic and technological landscapes, necessitating cost-effective strategies to ensure U.S. competitiveness. This research examines the economic feasibility of AI infrastructure development within the U.S., contrasting it with Chinese models and exploring strategies for optimizing cost efficiency. The paper provides a comprehensive analysis of cost structures, performance trade-offs, and infrastructure adaptation approaches, including cloud architecture modifications, hybrid open-source solutions, and AI workforce development. Furthermore, it evaluates governance, security, and regulatory considerations, emphasizing data privacy, compliance frameworks, and the role of federal agencies such as the FTC. The study also explores market dynamics, pricing innovations, and financial market responses to infrastructure shifts. Strategic recommendations focus on public-private collaborations, energy-efficient AI strategies, and investment models that drive infrastructure efficiency. Ultimately, this research advocates for a resilient, cost-effective AI ecosystem that aligns with U.S. national interests, technological leadership, and economic sustainability.  © 2025 IEEE.},
