	affiliations = {Harvard Extension School, Australia},
	abstract = {The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into reverse engineering has greatly enhanced the potency of cybersecurity, particularly in vulnerability discovery and software analysis. However, this progression has introduced ethical challenges, including the risk of AI tools being used inappropriately by malicious actors. This paper introduces the Responsible AI Reverse Engineering Framework (RAIRF), a comprehensive ethical framework which is designed to guide the safe and responsible application of AI in reverse engineering. RAIRF outlines three key principles: Ethical AI Training, Robust Data Governance, and Restricted Access Models, detailing specific scopes and elements for each. Through comprehensive qualitative case studies which utilize GPT 4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2, and Amazon Titan Text Embeddings applied to the same reverse engineering task, we compare their performance and suitability under the RAIRF principles. The framework emphasizes accountability, transparency, and purpose limitation, providing clear guidelines for how individuals and organizations can align AI advancements with ethical standards. © WCSE 2025. All rights reserved.},
