	journal = {Proceedings - 13th International Conference on Information Management and Engineering, ICIME 2025},
	pages = {160 - 166},
	doi = {10.1109/ICIME66598.2025.00037},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033875134?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {National University Philippines, School of Allied Health and Sciences, NU Lipa Lipa, Philippines},
	abstract = {The rapid advancements in artificial intelligence (AI) have introduced significant copyright concerns, particularly on the liabilities of AI actors in the unauthorized use of copyrighted materials as datasets for training AI applications. Given AI system's reliance on extensive datasets for machine learning, it is essential to examine the interplay between AI technologies and copyright laws to develop adaptive legal frameworks for the evolving landscape of creative expression and knowledge production. This study investigates global AI legislation in relation to copyright law, examines the permissible uses of copyrighted materials for training AI, and determines its limitations for AI development. This qualitative study employed a comparative analysis in examining international frameworks governing AI. Both primary and secondary authorities were utilized in this research, including the Intellectual Property Code of the Philippines (IPC), international copyright laws, treaties, legal instruments, books, legal journals, judicial decisions, and expert analyses. Findings reveal significant variations in the international AI regulations, with some jurisdictions implementing AI-specific provisions while others rely on existing copyright frameworks. AI legislation generally allows the use of copyrighted materials for training under certain conditions but lacks comprehensive guidelines on the liabilities of AI actors. The study highlights the following permissible uses of copyrighted content to balance innovation and protection of intellectual property rights: transparency exemptions, text and data mining, scientific and noncommercial research, personal enjoyment, computer data processing, incidental use, knowledge generation, and purposedivergent training use. The study also underscores the limitations on the use of protected contents for AI training including the option to reserve rights, maintain fair competition, proper attribution, avoidance of unreasonable harm, minimal use, and purpose different from the original work. To address gaps, the study recommends the development of a unified and clearer legal framework that explicitly addresses the use of copyrighted materials in AI training. It may include provisions on well-defined exceptions and limitations. These measures aim to balance innovation with protecting creators' rights, fostering a legally compliant and ethical AI ecosystem that respects intellectual property in the digital age.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AI development; AI legislation; AI training data; Artificial intelligence; copyright law; intellectual property; machine learning},
	keywords = {Data mining; Learning systems; Machine learning; Product liability; Artificial intelligence development; Artificial intelligence legislation; Artificial intelligence systems; Artificial intelligence training data; Copyright law; Copyrighted materials; Legal frameworks; Machine-learning; Property; Training data; Copyrights},
