	abstract = {A world characterized by multicultural diversity begs the question: Whose ethics should govern artificial intelligence (AI)? Exploring whether or not we need a universal ethical standard for AI, it is necessary to distinguish between ethics and morality. Dwelling on diverse philosophical traditions and insights from both Eastern and Western thinkers, the notion that AI should be regulated by global consensus is explored. Dharma (righteousness), a key tenet of Indic thought complementing views of Western luminaries bridges the East-West dichotomy, pointing to commonalities and divergences in designing ethical frameworks. To ground these theoretical perspectives in contemporary discourse, the chapter also features insights from modern thinkers and living masters. These voices offer practical reflections on how ancient wisdom can inform current AI ethics, advocating for a balanced approach that respects cultural diversity while seeking universal values. “Whose Ethics for AI?" argues that an overarching standard may be too idealistic and integrating diverse ethical philosophies can provide a more holistic and sustainable framework for AI development. Readers are invited to reconsider the topic of AI ethics. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Naresh Singh and Ram B. Ramachandran; individual chapters, the contributors.},
