	affiliations = {Sharda University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, U.P., Greater Noida, India},
	abstract = {The paper offers an in-depth, multidimensional analysis of human perceptions with regard to AI-generated digital avatars. Key themes involve the uncanny valley effect, perceived usefulness, ethical awareness, and cultural sensitivity. In all, 12 participants took part in the survey using a validated 23-item instrument whose reliability was excellent for all factors, Cronbach's α>0.78. Key findings show that ethical awareness coexists with, rather than opposes, technology acceptance. Regression modeling showed that age and experience with AI significantly predict perceptions of usefulness, R<sup>2</sup>=0.42, at a probability level below 0.05. Cluster analysis uncovered two distinct user clusters, namely, tech enthusiasts exhibiting high levels of acceptance and cautious evaluators preoccupied with cultural sensitivity. The correlations among factors reveal the role of ethical awareness as an acceptance moderator. These findings provide empirical guidance into culturally sensitive deployment and ethical governance of synthetic resurrection technologies. The currently conducted study falls within the scope of AI ethics, human-computer interaction, and policy development, further combining the approaches of psychological, utilitarian, normative, and contextual frames regarding the perception of synthetic avatars. © 2025 IEEE.},
