	abstract = {This chapter critically examines the entanglement of artificial intelligence(AI), cultural policy, and governance within the Creative and Cultural Industries(CCIs) and how this is shaping our understanding of interculturality. It argues that AI systems and their governance are deeply embedded in political-economic structures that privilege dominant logics while marginalizing pluralistic and non-Western epistemologies. Through comparative analysis of governance models—from neoliberal regimes to state-led developmental approaches—the chapter explores how AI is reconfiguring intercultural relations by mediating whose cultures are made visible, valued, or excluded in algorithmic systems. Framing AI both as a technological and intercultural actor, the chapter brings together the concepts of epistemic justice, digital sovereignty, and human capabilities to propose a critical agenda for cultural policy. It contends that equitable AI governance must foreground intercultural self-determination and actively support diversity, especially in digitally peripheral contexts, where AI risks entrenching existing hierarchies under the guise of innovation. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Fred Dervin and Hamza R’boul; individual chapters, the contributors.},
