	affiliations = {UNED, Spain; Diputación de Lugo, Spain},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) holds significant potential to revolutionise public administration by modernizing processes and enhancing service delivery. Nonetheless, it also introduces notable risks, such as reliance on external technology suppliers and the exacerbation of the digital divide within resource-limited local administrations. This article explores these challenges through a distinctive case study of the Provincial Council of Lugo (Spain), proposing a strategic model of sovereign AI based on open-source technologies. The research outlines a technological architecture designed to ensure data privacy and maintain institutional independence, along with a suite of applications addressing key public management challenges. The model features a provincial scalability plan rooted in the Platform as a Service (PaaS) paradigm, allowing the dissemination of technological benefits to municipalities with varying levels of capacity. Additionally, critical success factors are identified, including establishing a Competence Centre, enhancing digital literacy among public officials, and implementing a strong governance framework. In conclusion, the article demonstrates that technological sovereignty combined with a collaborative approach is vital for a fair and sustainable modernization of local administration. © 2025, Pablo Rivera Capón, José Antonio Arias Lombardero, Nicolás Fariña Verea},
