	abstract = {This chapter highlights the importance of axiological considerations in contemporary scientific debates on AI ethics. The research is framed from a cultural perspective, and a systematic literature review (using the SALSA model) was conducted based on a proposed set of original axiological domains. The study focuses on academic publications on AI ethics from the past five years. The results include: (1) the identification of key axiological “shifts” present in the literature – centered on five values: freedom, dignity, justice, safety, beneficence; and (2) the distinction of types of discourse on AI ethics across three disciplines: medicine; social sciences; and business, management, and accounting. This chapter aims to complement existing research on AI ethics by emphasizing the cultural conditions that shape the framework for defining the norms and principles governing AI. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Witold Abramowicz, Marek Kowalkiewicz and Krzysztof Węcel; individual chapters, the contributors.},
