	journal = {2025 IEEE MetroCon, Metrocon 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/Metrocon66796.2025.11405786},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036158601?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Electrical Engineering, University of North Texas, Denton, TX, United States},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence continues to expand across healthcare, energy, finance, and education, with global corporate investment exceeding $252 billion in 2024. Although recent advancements have benefited from increasingly large and complex models, continued scaling provides diminishing returns while placing growing demands on data and computational resources. Many limitations of modern AI systems arise from the data itself, including dataset bias, insufficient diversity, label errors, hidden subclasses, and improper train–test splits. These issues reduce reliability, distort evaluation, and limit real-world generalization. This paper summarizes a set of data-centric techniques that address these challenges. The methods include controlled data augmentation to increase meaningful variation, variance-aware dataset splitting to create balanced and representative evaluation sets, and dataset profiling to detect structural issues before training. Together, these approaches strengthen dataset quality and improve fairness, robustness, and transparency. They also support broader expectations for responsible AI development by providing clear mechanisms for improving the integrity of the data pipeline. Improving data quality provides a more effective path to trustworthy AI than continued model scaling. Data-centric design offers a practical foundation for building systems that generalize reliably, behave predictably, and meet the standards required for high-impact deployment. ©2025 IEEE.},
