	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036000915?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Swinburne University of Technology, Australia},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) continues to explode in its capabilities and adoption around the world, and the space sector is no exception to this trend. The plethora of applications for AI in the space sector, both upstream and downstream, also presents risks that must be managed where possible. The United Nations Space Treaties do not address AI as of yet, nor do national space law regimes. Consequently, the primary source of regulatory guidance for AI in the space sector is emerging legal frameworks intended to govern AI. Such frameworks are emerging from a variety of jurisdictions, with varying degrees of similarity shared among them. The ‘Brussels Effect’ describes the effect of European regulation on business practices in other jurisdictions, namely in instances where it may be more efficient and cost effective to maintain one system or process that complies with the more stringent EU regulations than multiple systems operating at varying standards of quality. Exemplifying this, the General Data Protection Regulation influenced the operation of organisations around the world with its stringent requirements regarding digital rights. With the advent of the EU’s AI Act, there has been discussion about whether the Brussels Effect would extend to the regulation of AI, in particular whether U.S AI companies will comply with the EU’s new rules out of necessity or convenience, or ignore them, disrupting the Brussels Effect. Recent policy changes and statements from the new U.S administration indicate a minimal approach to AI regulation is being pursued, standing in contrast to the broad scope of the EU’s AI Act. This puts the U.S and EU in a state of regulatory tension, raising questions as to whether the Brussels Effect will continue in the realm of AI. This paper explores the limits of the Brussels Effect and how this tension may affect organisations in the space sector. It examines the desirability of harmonised regulatory approaches to AI regulation in the context of the space sector, and the possible consequences of divergent approaches emerging. It contends that the benefits of compatible practices and risk management processes justify the alignment of practices between jurisdictions, regardless of broader political or cultural differences regarding AI regulation. It then calls for collaboration between agencies and industry to tailor standards and guidance appropriately for the space sector and the adoption of AI standards throughout the space sector. © 2025 by SmartSat CRC.},
	author_keywords = {AI Policy; Artificial Intelligence; AI Regulation; International Regulatory Harmonisation; International Standards; Space Law},
