	abstract = {With the increasing number of devices and users accessing the Internet, managing data transmission has emerged as a critical concern for both individuals and businesses. Path validation is the process of enforcing and then verifying a path during information transmission. Controlling the path data takes is crucial for ensuring digital sovereignty, establishing rightful control over assets like data, technology, and infrastructure. This not only affirms an entity's autonomy, security, and privacy in the digital realm, but also underscores the importance of regulating access and usage in accordance with local laws and regulations. Path validation has several applications, including traffic engineering, service chaining, and inter-domain routing. In this work, we systematize the knowledge surrounding path validation, particularly on the variants and extensions of the inter-domain routing protocol, the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP). We establish three classifications: one for attacks threatening the digital sovereignty of information transmission, another for path validation protocols, and the last for BGP variants and extensions. We analyse each solution against the selected attacks. Finally, we discuss open research problems towards achieving digital sovereignty during data transmission. © 2024 Elsevier B.V.},
