	affiliations = {Wuhan University, School of Information Management, Wuhan, China},
	abstract = {Human-AI collaborative rumor combating behavior refers to joint information-governance activities in which humans and artificial intelligence systems coordinate and complement each other to counter rumors and mitigate their negative impacts. Such decision-making is shaped not only by algorithmic performance and technical conditions but also by ethical norms and shared values. As a bidirectional ethical framework, human-AI interaction ethics comprises two dimensions: human ethics in human-AI interaction and AI ethics in AI-human interaction. Both dimensions critically influence collaborative rumor combating behavior. However, existing research on Human-AI collaborative rumorcombating behavior mainly focuses on specific stages of the rumor-combating process, such as rumor detection or verification, lacking systematic analysis and validated measurement scales. Moreover, prior research tends to overemphasize AI ethics in AI-human interaction while neglecting human ethics in human-AI interaction and their alignment effects on collaborative rumor combating behavior. This research adopts a dual-ethics perspective to explore the independent and joint effects of human and AI ethics on collaborative rumor combating behavior and to reveal their underlying mechanisms. The research aims to extend the theoretical boundary of human-AI collaboration and ethics research and offer practical insights for designing intelligent debunking systems and optimizing human-AI co-governance toward trustworthy and sustainable information governance. © 2025 IEEE.},
