	year = {2025},
	journal = {ICCR 2025 - 3rd International Conference on Cyber Resilience},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCR67387.2025.11292115},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031576018?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Imam Al-Kadhum College (IKU), Department of Computer Techniques Engineering, Baghdad, Iraq; Imam Al-Kadhum College (IKC), Computer Engineering Department, Al-Diwaniyah, Iraq; Imam Al-Kadhum College (IKC), Department of Cyber Security, Baghdad, 10011, Iraq; Baghdad College of Economic Sciences University, Department of English Language, Baghdad, 10011, Iraq; University of Baghdad, College of Languages, Department of English, Baghdad, 10011, Iraq; Imam Al-Kadhum College (IKC), Department of Computer Engineering Technology, Baghdad, 10011, Iraq},
	abstract = {The global change toward digital governance has amplified the strategic importance of critical infrastructure (CI) and its regulation under various international digital sovereignty frameworks. Political regimes, including democratic, authoritarian, and hybrid ones, play a decisive role in shaping these infrastructure strategies based on ideological, security, and economic priorities. However, existing methods often adopt binary or linear models that fail to account for the complex, multi-causal nature of regime behavior and policy outcomes in digital sovereignty. These limitations restrict a nuanced understanding of how diverse political and contextual factors interact to shape CI governance. This paper proposes the use of Fuzzy Set Qualitative Comparative Analysis (fsQCA) to overcome these issues by enabling the examination of causal configurations across multiple cases. fsQCA enables partial membership and assesses the interplay of political, economic, and technological factors that shape state strategies. Applying fsQCA to a cross-national sample, this research identifies multiple causal pathways through which different regime types adopt either high or low-intensity digital sovereignty strategies for CI protection. The findings reveal that no single factor determines strategy intensity; instead, regime type, cybersecurity threats, and foreign technology dependency interact in complex ways.  © 2025 IEEE.},
