	pages = {33 - 74},
	doi = {10.4018/979-8-3373-2484-5.ch002},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105025079418?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Wilhelm Büchner University of Applied Sciences, Germany},
	abstract = {In an era marked by systemic volatility, cognitive oversaturation, and the erosion of ethically grounded strategic reasoning, traditional leadership models—primarily reactive, mechanistic, and control-oriented—have reached both their epistemic and operational limits. This chapter introduces the Potentiality Metaheuristic, a transdisciplinary construct situated at the intersection of second-order cybernetics, infosomatic cognition, and anticipatory systems design. Rather than defining viability as performance optimization or risk containment, this model reinterprets it as the emergent coherence between ethically anchored self-regulation and context-sensitive enactment of autonomy. The Potentiality Metaheuristic is not a formalized equation, but an epistemic compass—a generative grammar for orienting systemic becoming under complexity. It is ontologically rigorous rather than technocratically reductive. Drawing from process philosophy, anticipatory theory, and cybernetic individuation, the chapter reconstructs the epistemic foundations of potentiality and introduces a generative heuristic built around four interdependent variables: Integrity, Autonomy, Contextual Resonance, and Dynamic Viability. These variables are not static traits but mutually constitutive fields of systemic intelligence—enabling transformation through recursive coherence, normative responsiveness, and ecological attunement. The metaheuristic's operational relevance is explored through simulations, strategic use cases, and foresight architectures in domains ranging from educational innovation and organizational redesign to AI ethics and civic governance. Rather than prescribing metrics, it enables sapient system design: the cultivation of systems capable of aligning latent capacities with emergent affordances in real time. Ultimately, the chapter contributes to a paradigmatic reorientation toward sapiocratic governance—a leadership logic grounded not in control, but in the ethical activation of potential. Here, autonomy is no longer framed as regulatory cost but as the generative precondition for systemic resilience, collective sense-making, and viable futures. © 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
	keywords = {Cognitive systems; Ecology; Ethical technology; Systems analysis; Anticipatory systems; Leadership model; Mechanistics; Metaheuristic; Operational limits; Oversaturation; Performance optimizations; Performance risk; Second orders; Strategic reasoning; Real time systems},
