	abstract = {The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and big data analytics (BDA) has brought about transformative advancements across sectors such as healthcare, finance, education, and transportation. However, these technologies also raise profound ethical challenges that demand critical examination. This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of the ethical considerations surrounding AI-enabled BDA, emphasizing issues of algorithmic bias, data privacy, accountability, transparency, human autonomy, job displacement, military applications, and environmental sustainability. Through in-depth analyses and real-world case studies—including the COMPAS criminal justice system, Amazon's hiring tool, and Clearview AI's facial recognition software—the chapter illustrates how unethical design and deployment can reinforce societal inequities and erode trust. Furthermore, it evaluates prominent ethical frameworks such as the EU AI Act, OECD AI Principles, and SDAIA AI Ethics Principles, highlighting their approaches to ethical governance in varying geopolitical contexts. The chapter concludes with a forward-looking discussion on emerging ethical concerns and research priorities, advocating for adaptive, inclusive, and globally informed AI governance to ensure the responsible evolution of smart systems. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
