	affiliations = {Shariah College, University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan; Brighton College Al Ain, Al Ain, United Arab Emirates; College of Education Humanities and Social Sciences, Al Ain University, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {This study explored the ethical implications of artificial intelligence (AI) from the perspective of Islamic jurisprudence. The rapid development of AI technologies and their integration into Muslim societies present both promising opportunities and challenges. While AI ethics and Islamic legal theories share fundamental principles of justice, fairness, and accountability, Western ethical frameworks often overlook religious and spiritual considerations that are central to Islamic teachings. The research highlighted critical concerns such as bias in AI algorithms, the role of AI in Fatwa issuance, and the potential ethical dilemmas associated with AI-enabled surveillance. Furthermore, it emphasized the need for collaboration between Islamic scholars, policymakers, and AI developers in developing governance frameworks for AI that are compliant with Shariah principles. By making AI development align with Islamic ethical standards, societies can harness its benefits while preserving human dignity and advancing justice. This study offered potential avenues for future research, including the development of complete regulatory frameworks for Islamic Artificial Intelligence, AI's effects on Islamic financial operations, and its contribution to governance and educational systems in Muslim countries.)  © 2025 IEEE.},
