	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) governance frameworks are increasingly used to ensure that emerging technologies are lawful, ethical, and robust. However, these frameworks often overlook structural issues of inclusivity and accessibility, particularly in Global South contexts where digital and institutional disparities persist. This paper critically examines the adequacy of three major AI assessment tools: ALTAI (Assessment List for Trustworthy AI), capAI (Conformity Assessment Procedure for AI System), and Meaningful Human Control (MHC), in evaluating public-sector AI deployments through the case of Indonesia's e-Tilang system, an AI-supported electronic traffic law enforcement program. Using a normative, case-driven analysis, this paper compares how each framework conceptualizes fairness, participation, and oversight. It finds that while ALTAI and capAI offer valuable procedural and ethical guidance, they often assume high levels of institutional maturity and digital literacy that do not reflect the realities of implementation in Indonesia. MHC contributes a crucial ethical lens but lacks operational clarity. To address these gaps, this paper introduces the ASEAN Guide on AI Governance and Ethics as a context-sensitive alternative and proposes a hybrid assessment model that combines ethical rigor with socio-technical relevance. This paper offers preliminary recommendations for developing more inclusive and responsive AI governance in Southeast Asia and other underrepresented regions. © 2025 IEEE.},
