	abstract = {This chapter argues that an equitable response to the financial and cultural upheavals caused by AI in music demands cohesive, value-driven leadership throughout the entire music ecosystem. Rooted in the idea of the “common good, " it reviews major AI ethics guidelines (from organisations such as the UN, EU, and IEEE) to formulate a standard set of “human-centred” principles. It then illustrates how these macro-level principles should be adapted into more nuanced, sector-specific approaches to music. Incorporating John Havens’ perspective on music and ethics from the IEEE, this chapter emphasises music’s inherently communal, transdisciplinary nature-where both data and cultural traditions function like notes and harmonies essential to a collective “groove.” However, AI frameworks often reflect predominantly Western viewpoints, risking the exclusion of diverse ethical traditions and voices. To address this gap, this chapter proposes practical steps such as a globally recognised AI “music mark, " an ethically grounded certification ensuring that AI tools for composition, distribution, and performance are transparent and accountable. Such a Trustmark, revised governance structures, and inclusive policymaking can help preserve the unique agency of creators and listeners. By safeguarding core human values-authenticity, data sovereignty, cultural plurality-within AI-driven music, we can support a sustainable and inclusive musical future for both human and nonhuman actors. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Martin Clancy; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Computer music; Ethical technology; Compositions distributions; Cultural traditions; Governance structures; Human values; Performance; Policy making; Value driven; Ecosystems},
