	abstract = {This paper presents an AI-powered framework for evaluating public sector debt and its macroeconomic implications across 37 OECD countries. Using Principal Component Analysis (PCA), K-means clustering, and ARIMAX forecasting, we identify seven significant predictors of public debt including inflation, unemployment, GDP per capita, and labor force participation. A debt risk scoring model ranks countries based on fiscal vulnerability to provide insight into long-term economic resilience. The framework integrates macro-fiscal data through 2024-2025 from OECD and IMF reports. Beyond analytics, the study addresses ethical and governance concerns in the use of AI for fiscal decision-making. Transparency, accountability, and fairness are examined alongside potential biases that may disadvantage developing economies. Recommendations are made for implementing explainable AI (XAI), regulatory compliance, and alignment with global AI governance frameworks. © 2025 IEEE.},
