	abstract = {Microsoft Power Platform has become a revolutionary initiative utilizing low-code/no-code (LCNC) and offering low-code access to IT experienced and less experienced institutions, enhanced application development, automation, and data-driven insight. The given paper explores two interdependent themes: (1) the role of Responsible Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Power Platform, and (2) security and governance implications of increased citizen development. Although AI-powered tools, such as Power Automate and Power BI, bring agility and improve decision-making processes in an organization, they have setbacks in the form of biasness, breach of data privacy, and the lack of clarity in how decisions are reached. Likewise the democratization of development by way of citizen users offers innovativeness at the same time evoking and posing issues in regard to shadow IT, lifecycle management and compliance. With the help of the scholarly literature, case studies of the enterprise, and architectural frameworks, the research outlines the urgent gaps of responsibility AI integration, governance, and risks mitigation methods. The paper also assesses how to implement the Responsible Design Patterns and XAI frameworks in the LCNC environment in an ethical life automation approach. These quantitative data on various aspects point at the productivity gains and failure of governance. The study is summarized with a series of practical research findings concerning integrating Responsible AI into the Power Platform workflows and institutionalizing of secure, scalable citizen development. Policymakers, enterprise architects, and business leaders can use these results when exploring the future of low-code automation based on AI-driven credibility.  © 2025 IEEE.},
