	affiliations = {Prince of Songkla University, Faculty of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Biomedical Engineering, Songkhla, 90110, Thailand},
	abstract = {This paper presents the development of a realtime drowsiness detection system tailored for elderly drivers, utilizing a dedicated AI chip for edge computing. Given the increasing number of aging drivers and their heightened vulnerability to fatigue-related accidents, the proposed solution aims to enhance road safety through timely and reliable detection of drowsiness symptoms. The system captures eye states via camera and processes data locally with a lightweight convolutional neural network (CNN), eliminating the need for cloud connectivity. The model was optimized and quantized for the MAX78000 AI microcontroller, leveraging its hardwareaccelerated CNN engine to achieve low-latency processing and ultra-low power consumption suitable for in-vehicle applications. Two models were trained and evaluated: one using a publicly available dataset from Hugging Face, and another using a smaller, custom-collected dataset gathered under controlled conditions with volunteers. Results show that the model trained on the Hugging Face dataset achieved higher accuracy due to the larger size and greater diversity of data. However, the model trained on the smaller, environmentspecific dataset still demonstrated acceptable performance for practical deployment, indicating that even with fewer images, reliable drowsiness detection can be achieved when training data reflects the intended application context.  © 2025 IEEE.},
