	abstract = {This paper examines the influence of Quality 5.0 management on the strengthening of technological sovereignty of high-tech productions and countries through collaboration with universities in the conditions of Industry 5.0. It is established that the modern notion of technological sovereignty goes beyond the purely economic competition and integrates the Sustainable Development Goals and aspects of safety and human-centredness. The concept of Quality 5.0, which combines proactive predictive analytics, socio-ethical, and environmental dimensions, as well as cyber resilience and interoperability, ensures a high level of adaptability and autonomy of production systems. This paper's methodological basis lies in an interdisciplinary combination of institutional analysis, the theory of technological transition, the concept of sustainable development, the systems theory, and quality management. We use a system approach, content analysis of scholarly sources and practical cases of collaboration between universities and high-tech companies, and the methods of analysis, synthesis, observation, and generalisation. The scientific novelty of this paper lies in the substantiation of a three-level model of the influence of Quality 5.0 on the formation of sustainable innovative ecosystems and an institutional infrastructure of technological sovereignty. We determine the main mechanisms of these ecosystems, in particular, universities, which are the core of intellectual capital, and high-tech productions, which perform the role of converters of scientific knowledge into practical soltuions and technologies, and characterise the main institutionalised forms of collaboration, which contribute to acceleration of knowledge transfer and ensure local autonomy of critical technologies. © 2025 Published by Faculty of Engineeringg.},
