	abstract = {The U.S. legal and regulatory structure is sectoral in nature and operates within an intricate federal and state framework. In the U.S. sectoral approach, health care, finance, education, and the federal government, among other sectors, are regulated separately, each with their own sets of laws and regulations at the federal and sometimes also at the state level. Examples of U.S. federal sectoral law include the health sector, regulated by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA). Another significant law in this sector is the Food, Drug and Cosmetics Administration Act which regulates pharmaceutical products and medical devices, among other areas. In the financial sector, major laws include the Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, among additional financial sector laws such as the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act of 1999. The education sector is partially regulated by the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act. Federal agency activities are subject to the Privacy Act of 1974 (Privacy Act). These are just a selection of the plethora of large federal sectoral laws © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
