	abstract = {Healthcare continues to grapple with a persistent gap between the promise of Responsible AI (RAI) guidelines and their amalgamation into everyday practice, particularly in establishing governance frameworks that can keep pace with swiftly progressing technologies. This study investigates how RAI shapes AI governance, considering algorithmic awareness as a potential mediator and organizational culture as a moderating force. Drawing on survey data from 347 respondents and analyzed using PLS-SEM, the results reveal that RAI strongly enhances governance outcomes, with organizational culture magnifying this effect, while algorithmic awareness does not serve a mediating role. Grounded in the AI4people framework, the Technology-Organization-Environment (TOE) framework, and Human-Computer Trust theory, the study offers actionable insights for policymakers and healthcare leaders to strengthen oversight, build trust, and ensure regulatory alignment. As an original contribution, it provides an empirical basis for advancing sustainable AI governance in diverse and evolving healthcare environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
