	affiliations = {Graduate School of Business Sciences, University of Tsukuba, Tokyo, Japan},
	abstract = {Large-scale data collection in marketing research faces increasing constraints due to rising costs and strengthened privacy regulations, and cross-border management of actual data are also challenging. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as promising alternatives for simulating consumer behavior, concerns remain regarding their alignment with human responses and the opacity of their reasoning processes. Informed by Data Free Flow with Trust (DFFT)-an international data governance principle emphasizing transparency, privacy protection, and interoperability-this study proposes a system framework that integrates synthetic data generated from demographic and psychometric data with LLMs via the Model Context Protocol (MCP). We empirically compared human survey responses, standalone LLM outputs, and MCP-integrated LLM outputs in rank-order tasks within conjoint analysis, a statistical method for consumer preference estimation. Results reveal that LLM-based preference estimations significantly diverge from human responses even when integrated with synthetic data, clarifying the current limitations of LLM-based consumer simulation. However, the MCP-based architecture provides transparency through explicit data provenance tracking, privacy protection through synthetic data utilization, and interoperability via standardized protocols, demonstrating alignment with DFFT principles. This study presents a verifiable technical configuration of AI infrastructure while elucidating boundary conditions for LLM application in consumer research. © 2025 IEEE.},
