	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) has fundamentally transformed cybersecurity in the banking sector, simultaneously providing advanced defensive capabilities and introducing new forms of risk. AI enables real-time detection of cyber-attacks, flags fraudulent financial activity by recognizing behavioral patterns, and automates threat responses. Key technologies like machine learning, natural language processing, and predictive analytics safeguard sensitive transactions and enhance resilience. However, these same advances empower cybercriminals, who exploit adversarial AI, deepfakes, and sophisticated phishing techniques to circumvent defenses and manipulate financial systems. Challenges such as data privacy, model explainability, algorithmic bias, and compliance with evolving regulations (e.g., GDPR, SOX, AI Act) further complicate AI adoption in banks. The growing ethical imperative mandates transparency, fairness, and accountability, especially in high stakes applications like credit scoring, anti-money laundering, and fraud detection. This article explores the dualistic role of AI in financial cybersecurity, the associated ethical and regulatory challenges, and advocates for a robust governance framework. Proposed measures include human-in-the-loop oversight, adversarial testing, continuous monitoring, and adherence to ethical standards. The future of AI in this domain hinges on striking a balance between leveraging innovation for protection and containing the evolving risks through proactive, multidisciplinary oversight and collaboration. © 2025 IEEE.},
