	abstract = {This Innovative Practice paper presents the STRAKER (Student Training in Responsible AI Knowledge and Ethics Research) Project, an approach designed to teach AI ethics in a more holistic and integrated way. This paper begins by providing background on the STRAKER project, offering a critical overview of existing approaches to technology and engineering ethics education, along with the limitations of these methods. It then briefly introduces the APPE IEB model, highlighting its key features and reviewing existing work on its use as a formal ethics pedagogy in the classroom. Next, the paper presents the STRAKER project, which aims to help faculty across various disciplines build the expertise and capacity needed to transform the APPE IEB model into an integrative and engaged AI ethics pedagogy in their classrooms. Major components of the STRAKER project are also introduced, including faculty training activities focused on (1) case writing, (2) development of instructional guides, and (3) designing classroom learning and assessment activities. Finally, the paper reflects on the opportunities and challenges in the implementation of the STRAKER project. This paper contributes to the underexplored scholarship on research informed and systematic approaches to AI ethics pedagogies, despite the growing recognition among engineering programs of the urgency and value of teaching AI ethics, as well as the extensive body of research on AI ethics. © 2025 IEEE.},
