	abstract = {Ensuring the integrity of AI-driven assessment and fostering trust in AI frameworks for online education is critical for learners and institutions issuing digital credentials. Challenges arise from learners exploiting system vulnerabilities and from algorithmic biases in ML models evaluating outcomes, performance, grades, and engagement, which can compromise fairness and reliability. This paper introduces an ethical process framework called MoSART (Modeling Trust in Student Learning and Analytical Results), a dialogue-based System that guides educational decision-makers in building trustworthy AI systems through automated bias testing in a human-understandable way. MoSART formalizes an end-to-end workflow from stakeholder objectives to three key classes: (i) Data- including data instance contingencies, data provenance, labeling processes, and attribute statistics, (ii) ML Models- the machine learning models applied along with their configurations and parameters, (iii) Learning Outcomes- the results, performance metrics, and logical arguments linking data and model outputs to decisions. The framework integrates automated bias testing and learner profiling to ensure fairness, and transparency. To highlight our approach and demonstrate its usefulness, the MoSART framework is implemented with a supporting tool, along with a representative usage scenario. © 2025 IEEE.},
