	doi = {10.1201/9781003585527-10},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016584089?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This chapter explores the intersection of Christian theological concepts and contemporary transhumanist ideas, particularly focusing on the theme of deification (theosis) and its relevance to the discourse on artificial intelligence and some of its possible consequences for the future of humanity. With the notion of the deification of the human, fundamental questions about human identity, purpose, morality, and the ethical implications of advancing artificial intelligence (AI) technologies are addressed. The analysis confronts the transhumanist narrative of human obsolescence and the prospect of machine morality surpassing the human. In contrast, an alternative AI ethics framework grounded in the theological theme of deification is proposed. This is structured around four facets: (1) vital embodiment and human technology-relations, (2) myth and meaning, (3) the question of power, and (4) a concept of sovereign theonics. Through this framework, a vision of an ethos of deification is unfolded, which, on the one hand, frees the human from the constraints of technology and, on the other, argues in favor of a vision of AI development that respects and enhances human dignity and potential by integrating deeply rooted religious-philosophical perspectives into the broader discourse on AI ethics. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Naresh Singh and Ram B. Ramachandran; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Ethical technology; Artificial intelligence technologies; Christians; Ethical implications; Hands-free; Human identity; Power; Transhumanists; Artificial intelligence},
