	affiliations = {Faculty of Science & Humanities, Poornima University, Rajasthan, Jaipur, India; Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Poornima University, Rajasthan, Jaipur, India; Department of Automat ionBanasthali Vidyapith, Rajasthan, India},
	abstract = {This chapter would discuss the ethical issues involving the 6G technology in smart cities by raising several concerns such as data privacy, AI governance, social inclusion, and equitable access. So positive 6G is promising transformed advances in ultra-low latency and unparalleled connectivity with advanced AI capacities and challenges it presents, like safeguarding personal freedom, fair benefit redistribution, algorithmic risk mitigation, and possibility of socioeconomic divisions. This makes for a raft of critical questions about the autonomy, transparency, and accountability in cities. It will, therefore, bring forth the call for all-rounded ethical frameworks that would guide the development and governance of AI-integrated systems within 6G-enabled cities. This will safeguard the rights of the individual, restrain algorithmic bias, and open up avenues for accountability over the potential undesirable impacts. As 6G technologies enable innovations such as real-time holographic communication and brain–computer interfaces, ethical concerns will stretch into cognitive privacy, digital identity, and human agency. This study further argues for co-governance structures that ought to involve diverse stakeholders: policymakers, technologists, urban planners, and citizens. Including stakeholders in an anticipatory fashion is important to deal with the ethical challenges in a manner that is innovation-friendly, which in turn honors the values of the society such that the roll-out of 6G technology would be integrative and not add to existing inequality or establish new exclusions. The next chapter reveals that embedding ethics into 6G-enabled smart cities could facilitate sustainable and inclusive urban development. Responsible uses of 6G capabilities might assist city planners in building smart ecosystems aimed at growing environmental sustainability while optimizing resources to support a high quality of life. Such in-ethics, therefore, act as a firewall against the risks of emerging technologies and an instrument to turn urban spaces into humanity-centered and responsible environments. This chapter, through investigation, provides insights and recommendations for incorporation of ethics into smart cities and previews the future of fair and sustainable urban development with prospects offered by 6G technology. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Sushil Kumar Singh, Rajendrasinh B. Jadeja, Ashish Khanna, Pushan Kumar Dutta, and Bal Virdee; individual chapters, the contributors.},
