	journal = {International Conference on Computer and Applications, ICCA 2025 - Proceedings},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCA66035.2025.11430762},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036978111?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Arab Open University, Fcs, Muscat, Oman; Minia University, Faculty of Science, Cs Department, Minia, Egypt},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) increasingly mediates children's lives in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) across learning, play, and social participation. Consent-centric regimes, such as COPPA and general data-protection statutes, do not reliably govern design harms - including profiling by default, geolocation defaults, engagement-maximizing nudges, or opaque inference - salient to AI-enabled services. We ask: What childspecific AI safeguards are missing in MENA law and practice, and which enforceable standards are feasible for the region?We conduct a comparative analysis of major frameworks - including COPPA, GDPR (children), and the UK's Age-Appropriate Design Code (AADC) - and evaluate them against MENA-salient risks like Arabic dialect moderation gaps and manipulative design. Our findings reveal systematic gaps concerning default settings, profiling, and regionally-aware safety testing, highlighting that AADC-style obligations offer the most translatable foundation for the MENA context. Based on this analysis, we propose the MENA Age-Appropriate AI Design Code, a twelve-standard framework that mandates safety-by-design. This code provides a concrete, enforceable model for protecting children by shifting focus from parental consent to provider accountability. © 2025 IEEE.},
