	abstract = {Ensuring academic integrity in large-scale online examinations is a critical challenge for distance education institutions. This paper presents the design and evaluation of an AI-driven proctoring system integrating facial recognition, eye-tracking, and behavioral analytics to monitor misconduct during online examinations. The system was deployed across more than 100 subjects with 2,700 (AI = 2,000; Ruled-based automated proctoring = 700) candidates in an open and distance learning (ODL) university. Data sources included system-generated logs, institutional exam records, and post-exam student surveys. Results indicate that the system reduced suspected incidents of misconduct by 20-25% compared to Ruled-based automated proctoring, while enabling scalability across multiple disciplines. Despite these improvements, student feedback highlighted concerns regarding privacy, continuous surveillance, and algorithmic fairness. To address these issues, the study introduces a responsible proctoring framework emphasizing transparency, data protection, bias mitigation, and accountability. The study highlights the need for responsible AI adoption in digital examinations, offering empirical evidence and a practical framework to guide institutions towards fair, scalable, and ethically grounded assessment models.  © 2025 IEEE.},
