	abstract = {This chapter examines the emerging media ethics surrounding generative AI in Southern African newsrooms: South Africa, Zimbabwe, Zambia, and Eswatini. While generative AI is on the rise, the study argues that newsrooms in the sub-region should prioritise traditional journalistic practices and human oversight to mitigate the risks of over-reliance on this technology. This study distinguishes itself through its Africa-centric approach to generative AI ethics. Through content analysis and interviews, informed by a deontological framework, the study reveals increasingly prominent ethical concerns. These include the proliferation of misinformation and “fake news, " declining research and interviewing standards, inadequate attribution and substantiation, copyright infringement, and privacy violations. It addresses questions: How do Southern African media outlets disclose their use of generative AI in news gathering and writing? What elements of Southern African media industry codes pertain to generative AI use? How do journalists and media experts perceive the ethical implications of using generative AI in news reporting? What emerging patterns and themes regarding the appropriateness of generative AI use are evident in Southern African media codes? The findings indicate a lack of transparency regarding generative AI usage in Southern African media outlets’ news gathering and writing processes. AI guidelines are absent from media codes in all investigated countries except South Africa. Adherence to guidelines is opaque, raising concerns about manipulation and misinformation when generative AI operates without human oversight. This situation is further complicated by existing ethical dilemmas related to social media and declining advertising and newspaper sales revenues due to competition from big tech companies like Meta, X, and Google. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Lungile A. Tshuma and Trust Matsilele.},
