	affiliations = {University of Miami School of Law, Coral Gables, FL, United States},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is no longer confined to Earth, but existing legal frameworks remain focused on terrestrial applications. The 2024 European Union AI Act’s focus on terrestrial applications leaves regulatory gaps in AI-driven space technologies, particularly high-resolution satellite imaging with biometric tracking and behavioural analysis, leading to enforcement ambiguities and concerns over privacy, function creep, and oversight. While courts may interpret the AI Act’s applicability to satellite-based AI, judicial rulings cannot replace proactive legislation defining regulatory boundaries and enforcement. Proposed amendments include automatic blurring software and a rigorous licensing process to ensure ethical AI use. Two international regulatory approaches are examined: one establishing a centralised International Space AI Regulatory Authority (ISARA)—modelled after the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) and the International Maritime Organization (IMO)—and a fragmented model where nations develop independent regulations. This analysis aims to enhance collaboration, address regulatory gaps, and ensure responsible AI deployment in space. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
