	abstract = {Deep generative models (DGMs) are rapidly transforming healthcare by enabling new diagnostics, personalized treatments, and drug discovery applications. This chapter discusses the distinctive ethical and legal challenges that are raised by the use of DGMs in healthcare settings. Although DGMs offer the potential for improved clinical performance, they are associated with substantial challenges, such as data privacy, informed consent, intellectual property protection, discrimination by algorithms and accountability for error. Through a doctrinal research methodology, the paper examines existing legal provisions, ethical codes, and international examples, demonstrating the deficiencies in laws that affect the fulfilment of this right. This highlights the need for strong regulatory frameworks and interdisciplinary collaboration to mitigate the opacity of DGMs and safeguard transparency, justice and accountability. Highlights policy recommendations to build from strengths by strengthening explainable AI systems, adapting intellectual property law for AI-generated innovations, and promoting equity to avert gaps in healthcare. The chapter proposes a multi-stakeholder, ethical framework with the objective of providing a framework to balance innovation with societal value to arrive at responsible AI-driven healthcare advances. © 2026 Balasubramaniam S and Seifedine Kadry.},
