	abstract = {This chapter provides an in-depth exploration of the ethical dimensions of artificial intelligence (AI) integration in healthcare. It begins by discussing the increasing role of AI technologies in diagnostics, clinical decision-making, and personalized treatments, while highlighting key ethical challenges that arise from their application. The chapter introduces the main schools of ethical thought-utilitarianism, rights-based ethics, and virtue ethics-and how these frameworks can govern the ethical use of AI in healthcare. The principles of autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, and transparency are emphasized as essential components in developing trustworthy AI systems. Case studies are provided to demonstrate both the potential benefits of AI and the ethical dilemmas they pose, such as privacy concerns and the potential for bias. Finally, the chapter examines future directions for creating adaptable and effective ethical frameworks that evolve alongside AI technologies, ensuring fairness, accountability, and the safeguarding of patient welfare. Through a comprehensive review of AI ethics, this chapter contributes to the broader conversation on fostering collaboration between healthcare professionals, technologists, and policymakers to maximize the benefits of AI while mitigating its risks. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Anoop V.S., Suhasini Verma, Usharani Hareesh Govindarajan.},
