	abstract = {Procure-to-Pay (P2P) automation is essential for enhancing overall enterprise efficiency. Yet, existing systems face several challenges such as inconsistent policy enforcement, audit readiness, and operational inefficiencies, especially in multistakeholder environments. These issues and complexities hinder smooth integration and effective process optimization. Current research lacks a comprehensive framework to address these issues in a fully scalable, adaptable, and operationally sound manner. This study designs and validates a novel governance-aligned AI framework that integrates policy-as-code, object-centric analysis, and continuous monitoring to ensure consistent policy enforcement, compliance, and audit readiness. The methodology combines structured stakeholder interviews, an integrative literature review, and systematic model development, followed by a rigorous and thorough proof-of-concept evaluation using synthetic P2P scenarios. Performance results from 30 independent trials show two key findings: the average decision latency is 128.4 milliseconds (σ = 7.6ms), indicating stable performance even under high load, and the framework ensures consistent policy enforcement while providing a tamper-proof audit trail, effectively preventing errors such as double-counting and inconsistent exception handling. These results validate the framework's operational effectiveness and provide a robust, reliable foundation for implementing governance-aligned P2P automation, indicating readiness for real-world deployment across diverse organizational contexts and offering practical insights for future scalability and enhanced policy design. © 2025 IEEE.},
