	abstract = {This chapter explores governance dilemmas that emerge when law enforcement agencies deploy facial recognition technologies, focusing on the case of the Swedish Police Authority’s use of Clearview AI. Through a detailed analysis of legal, organizational and technological tensions, this chapter identifies four key tensions: efficiency vs. privacy; professional discretion vs. organizational responsibility; lack of control over technology; and institutional misconceptions about the technology. The case illustrates the complex challenges public authorities face when deploying powerful surveillance technologies developed by private companies beyond EU jurisdiction. Particular attention is given to the difficulty of complying with data protection laws, especially the right to erasure, in relation to emerging AI systems. By unpacking how control, power, legitimacy and accountability are negotiated in this context, this chapter contributes with critical insights into the broader implications of algorithmic governance for police legitimacy and democratic rights. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Einar Iveroth, Jan Lindvall and Johan Magnusson; individual chapters, the contributors.},
