	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence systems are increasingly being deployed in medical, financial and independent systems, where both reliability and transparency are paramount. This paper introduces a general framework that integrates measurable trustworthiness factors such as fairness, robustness, reliability and accountability with novel explainability methods such as interpretable models and post-hoc methods such as LIME and SHAP. By leveraging data from existing studies, and modifying already proven open-source tools, the framework is presented in a modular prototype with the purpose of enhancing clarity and ethical standards in decision-making with AI. As well as aiming for strong predictive performance, the purpose of the framework is to reduce biases and increase the protection from adversarial inputs. Its effectiveness is measured against benchmarks reported from the previous research. This approach offers a practical solution to creating AI systems that are technically good, while providing the opportunity to comply with ethics in order to support user trust, while adhering to regulations, especially in sensitive fields. © 2025 IEEE.},
