	abstract = {This conceptual paper presents an equity-first framework for AI-enabled student advising intended to expand fair access to international higher education. Addressing three persistent barriers - information asymmetry, prohibitive advising costs, and the absence of individualized guidance - the model integrates a dynamic recommendation engine, automated application support, and multilingual information services within a cost-sensitive, scalable architecture. Distinctively, the framework operationalizes ethics through three interlocking safeguards: algorithmic fairness audits and impact assessments; transparent data governance with consent and accountability mechanisms; and inclusive, low-bandwidth user experience aligned with universal design for learning. We outline a phased deployment strategy for under-resourced contexts (pilot → evaluate → scale) and specify evaluation indicators that foreground equity outcomes rather than throughput alone. The paper contributes (1) an explicit equity objective for AI-driven recommendations, (2) a public-interest access model, and (3) an actionable policy and governance playbook for institutions and governments. We argue that ethically grounded implementation and cross-sector collaboration are prerequisites for ensuring AI reduces, rather than reproduces, structural inequities in global higher education.  © 2025 IEEE.},
