	affiliations = {University of Helsinki, Finland; University of Helsinki, Finland},
	abstract = {‘Human-centric AI’ has become a core concept of responsible algorithmic governance. Digital transformation is associated with the complexification of governance, with questions such as how to regulate automation technologies due to the ‘black-box’ nature of AI. Moreover, the use of algorithms is blurring human agency in public decision-making, raising the question of accountability. The notion of ‘human-centric AI’ is proposed as a means for empowering people amidst the increasing automation of society. In governance strategies, human-centricity is portrayed as a design principle to break from ‘organisation-centricity’. We analyse human-centricity as an instance of hybridity, implying public and private actors as well as human and non-human agency. Our analysis shows paradoxical features of human-centricity in Finnish digital governance. Instead of enhancing accountability, human-centricity is used as an argument for designing seamless rapid service allocation in public governance for the individual citizen or removing organisational boundaries (both public and private), hence blurring accountability relations. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025},
