	abstract = {Veritistic governance in science marginalises epistemic minorities, such as TCM practitioners and Global South scholars, through normative lock-in, gatekeeping, and myopia. This paper introduces Artificiality, a phronetic framework integrating virtue epistemology, STS, and design ethics. It recalibrates epistemic systems via transparency, accountability, inclusivity, adaptability, and reproducibility. Dynamic audits and trading-zone protocols ensure context-sensitive governance. Pilot tests, like a 15% reduction in unjust rejections at X Journal and a 40% decrease in hospital stays in TCM-Western trials, show improved fairness and quality. Applications in AI ethics (e.g. a 30% bias reduction) and climate science (e.g. integrating Indigenous knowledge systems) demonstrate its potential to reduce bias and foster pluralism, despite challenges like audit fatigue. Artificiality reframes objectivity as reflexive pluralism for diverse, ethical domains. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
