	affiliations = {National School of Applied Sciences, Ibn Tofail University, Kenitra, Morocco},
	abstract = {Reskilling and upskilling are essential to national digital strategies; however, the adoption of artificial intelligence introduces new risks, including bias, opacity, and the potential for data abuse. This paper proposes a foreseeable AI governance model for large-scale training and capacity-building projects. We debate about whether the current strategy (incorporating procurement procedures with multi-actors monitoring KPIs) can be applied for AI tools in Estimating, Adaptive Learning, and Validating processes. The framework is organised around three governance dimensions (procurement, performance, and multi-stakeholder). Next, we consider three levels of governance maturity (no governance, minimal governance, and full governance) as an example of how these pillars might be implemented with varying rigor in order to demonstrate their potential impact on trust, employability, and equity. The paper provides prescience for policymakers and training professionals, contending that robust AI governance is a prerequisite not just for upskilling the incumbent workforce hut also for reskilling non-digital workers to secure digital employment. © 2025 IEEE.},
